'''Balæninæ''' (bal-ẹ̄-nī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Balæna'' + ''-inæ''.] A subfamily of ''Balænidæ'', typified by the genus ''Balæna'', containing only the smooth right whales. See ''Balænidæ''.

'''Balænoidea''' (bal-ẹ̄-noi′dẹ̄-ä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Balæna'' + ''-oidea''.] One of the three primary groups into which the ''Cetacea'' are divisible, the other two being the ''Delphinoidea'' and the ''Phocodontia''. It embraces the right whales (''Balæna'') and the fin-whales (''Balænoptera'', etc.).

'''Balænoptera''' (bal-ẹ̄-nop′tẹ-rä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''balæna'', a whale, + Gr. πτερόν, a wing.] A genus of whalebone whales, containing the several species of piked whales, rorquals, finners, finbacks, or razor-backs, so called from their long, sharp, falcate dorsal fin. {{smaller|They are found in all seas. Some are very large, as ''B. sibbaldi'', which attains a length of 80 feet. The flippers have 4 digits; the baleen is short and coarse; the skin of the throat is folded; the head is small, flat, and pointed; the body is long and slender; and the cervical vertebræ are free. Common Atlan-}}

